{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-220-221", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-05-12-solomons-porch-gathering", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 20.220-221: the eastern cloisters of the outer court, which the people persuaded Agrippa to rebuild, were remembered as 'the work of king Solomon' - the temple colonnade tradition ascribed to Solomon, the same setting Acts 5:12 names; it attests the portico and its attribution, not that the believers gathered there."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-220-221", "type": "attests", "to": "place:solomons-porch", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus records the eastern cloister of the temple as the colonnade 'the work of king Solomon' - the porch's location along the temple's eastern side and its Solomonic attribution; he does not use the name 'Solomon's porch'."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-05-12-solomons-porch-gathering", "grade": "B", "note": "Herod's temple stood in Jerusalem through the 30s CE with its colonnades; it attests the setting, not this gathering."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "place:solomons-porch", "grade": "B", "note": "The Second Temple esplanade with its porticoes stood in Acts's period; it attests the institution of temple colonnades, not the identification of this exact span."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-sects", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-05-17-sadducee-sect", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus: 'the Jews had for a great while had three sects of philosophy... the sect of the Essens, and the sect of the Sadducees, and the third sort of opinions was that of those called Pharisees' - the sect of the Sadducees that Acts 5:17 names was a real party of the period. Moved by reviewer 10 from the rising claim, which the evidence does not attest (bundled-claim split, per the Acts 17 politarchs model)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-199-sanhedrin", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-05-17-sadducees-high-priest-party", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 20.9.1 (62 CE): the high priest Ananus 'was also of the sect of the Sadducees' - a sitting high priest belonging to the Sadducees a generation after Acts 5; it attests the association of the high-priestly circle with that sect, not this scene."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-199-sanhedrin", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-05-21-council-and-senate", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: in 62 CE Ananus 'assembled the sanhedrim of judges' in Jerusalem - the same high-priestly council convening in the same city a generation later; it attests the institution, not this session."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-middot-1-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-04-01-captain-of-the-temple", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Middot 1:1: 'the officer of the Temple Mount used to go round to every watch' and beat sleeping watchers - an officer over the temple night watch. Redacted c. 200 CE and possibly the 'man of the temple mount' rather than the captain of the temple proper, so context for the office, not proof of it. Retargeted to the office claim (Acts 4:1) when the Acts 5:24 event was split off per the architect's titles-and-offices ruling (2026-11-15)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-6-288-300", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-05-26-captain-no-violence", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus shows the captain of the temple leading temple men in a tense gate-keeping action before 70 CE - the kind of command the office carried; it attests the office's conduct, not this arrest."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-sotah-9-15", "type": "attests", "to": "person:gamaliel-the-elder", "grade": "A", "note": "Mishnah Sotah 9:15: 'from the time when Rabban Gamliel the Elder died, the honor of the Torah ceased' - an independent rabbinic source names the same renowned Torah authority Acts 5:34 presents as a teacher honored by all the people. The Mishnah was redacted c. 200 CE, so it preserves later memory of his standing and may not describe the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-gittin-4-2-3", "type": "attests", "to": "person:gamaliel-the-elder", "grade": "A", "note": "Mishnah Gittin 4:2-3 records several legal ordinances 'Rabban Gamliel the Elder instituted... for the betterment of the world' - the same legislating sage of the early first century. Redacted c. 200 CE; may not describe the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-theudas", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-05-36-theudas-revolt", "grade": "D", "note": "Josephus (Ant. 20.97) records a Theudas who 'told them he was a prophet', persuaded a great part of the people to follow him to the Jordan, and was slain with his following dispersed - the same name, story shape and fate Acts 5:36 reports. Graded D because the identification is disputed: Josephus dates it c. 44-46 CE under Fadus, after Gamaliel's speech (about 34 CE), and has 'a great part of the people' rather than about four hundred men."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-1-taxation", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-05-37-judas-galilee-enrollment", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 18.1.1: 'one Judas, a Gaulonite, of a city whose name was Gamala', with Sadduc a Pharisee, led a revolt when Cyrenius (Quirinius) came into Judea to tax it (6 CE) - the same man (elsewhere 'Judas the Galilean') and the same census-registered revolt Acts 5:37 dates to 'the days of the enrollment'."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-zealots", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-05-37-judas-galilee-enrollment", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 18.1.6: 'of the fourth sect of Jewish philosophy, Judas the Galilean was the author' - the same man named as founder of a lasting movement that began at the census."}
{"from": "artifact:quirinius-syria-coins", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-05-37-judas-galilee-enrollment", "grade": "B", "note": "Coins issued by Quirinius's administration in Syria, inscribed with 'the 36th year of Caesar [Augustus]' (5/6 CE), attest Quirinius governing Syria in the census period - the setting of 'the days of the enrollment'; they do not attest Judas himself."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-makkot-3-10", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-05-40-apostles-flogged", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Makkot 3:10 fixes court flogging at 'forty lashes less one' and describes the procedure - flogging was a real judicial punishment of the council's tradition. Redacted c. 200 CE; it may not describe the Sanhedrin's practice of the 30s CE exactly, and it does not say the apostles received thirty-nine lashes."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-theudas", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:ac-05-36-theudas-revolt", "note": "Josephus dates Theudas's revolt to c. 44-46 CE (while Fadus was procurator), over a decade after Gamaliel's speech (about 34 CE, Caiaphas's tenure) - so Acts's 'before these days' cannot describe Josephus's Theudas. Explanations, notes only: an earlier, otherwise unknown Theudas, or Luke's chronological error."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-1-taxation", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:ac-05-36-theudas-revolt", "note": "Acts puts Judas of Galilee after Theudas ('After this man...'), but Josephus places Judas's revolt at the census of Quirinius in 6 CE, decades before his Theudas (c. 44-46 CE) - the second chronological tension of the speech."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-theudas", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:ac-05-37-judas-galilee-enrollment", "note": "Acts sequences Judas after Theudas; Josephus sequences Theudas (c. 44-46 CE) after Judas (6 CE). One of the two orders must be wrong."}
{"from": "claim:ac-05-24-captain-heard-report", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-22-52-captains-arrest", "note": "Acts 5:24 and 26 use the singular captain of the temple; Luke 22:52 the plural captains of the temple coming with the chief priests - the same office in two different events."}
{"from": "claim:ac-05-37-judas-galilee-enrollment", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-02-02-enrollment-timing", "note": "Both verses turn on Quirinius's enrollment: Acts 5:37 dates Judas the Galilean's revolt to it (6 CE per Josephus), Luke 2:2 ties the enrollment to Jesus's birth - the dating tension of Luke 2:2 stands on the same census."}
